<PubmedArticleSet>
<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">35061894</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2022</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>02</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2022</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>02</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1939-327X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>71</Volume><Issue>4</Issue><PubDate><Year>2022</Year><Month>Apr</Month><Day>01</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Diabetes</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Diabetes</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Randomized Controlled Trial of the Hemodynamic Effects of Empagliflozin in Patients With Type 2 Diabetes at High Cardiovascular Risk: The SIMPLE Trial.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>812</StartPage><EndPage>820</EndPage><MedlinePgn>812-820</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2337/db21-0721</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Treatment with the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor (SGLT-2i) empagliflozin significantly reduces cardiovascular events in patients with type 2 diabetes (T2D); however, the mechanisms behind the reduction in cardiovascular (CV) events are unknown. We investigated whether SGLT-2i treatment affected central hemodynamics during rest and exercise in 34 patients with diabetes in this investigator-initiated, randomized, placebo-controlled, double-blinded trial. The primary end point was change in pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) at a submaximal ergometer workload (25 W) after 13 weeks of SGLT-2i treatment (25 mg once daily) compared with placebo. Secondary end points included changes in resting hemodynamics. Baseline and follow-up hemodynamic assessments were performed at rest, submaximal exercise (25 W), and peak exercise using right heart catheterization. Treatment with empagliflozin for 13 weeks in patients with T2D at high CV risk did not reduce left heart filling pressure more than placebo at submaximal exercise. At rest, we observed that empagliflozin reduced PCWP at a magnitude of clinical significance.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2022 by the American Diabetes Association.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wolsk</LastName><ForeName>Emil</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6064-062X</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Herlev and Gentofte Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>J&#xfc;rgens</LastName><ForeName>Mikkel</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Endocrinology, Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Schou</LastName><ForeName>Morten</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Herlev and Gentofte Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ersb&#xf8;ll</LastName><ForeName>Mads</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Herlev and Gentofte Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hasbak</LastName><ForeName>Philip</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Clinical Physiology, Nuclear Medicine &amp; PET and Cluster of Molecular Imaging, Rigshospitalet and University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kj&#xe6;r</LastName><ForeName>Andreas</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Clinical Physiology, Nuclear Medicine &amp; PET and Cluster of Molecular Imaging, Rigshospitalet and University of Copenhagen, Copen